4. What this means for the draft
Supports L5 (costly end). In every factory-equipment line, and in pumps and compressors, China's share of weight is 15 to 30 points above its share of value. The gap held or widened from 2019 to 2025. This is the backbone exhibit for Chapter 6.
Exceptions confirmed. Gensets: in Thailand, Chinese large gensets cost about the same per kg as established ones (1.1x), and China's share in India jumped from 20% to 44% in 2025 as established makers sold out (Ch 3, L2). Excavators in Thailand: no price gap at all.
Robots are moving up. Indonesia's robot price gap narrowed from 3.9x to 2.3x, and China's share went from 17% to 62% in one year. This fits L6 (new factories, new kind of machine). Check what drove 2025 before using it.
Flag for Ch 3. The Thai excavator "no gap, little moves" example weakens with 2025: China 35→42%, Japan 40→44%. Either keep the 2019–24 window and say so, or swap in JCB backhoes in India as the main "no gap" case.
New point for Ch 5. India is the only market where China's share fell broadly in 2025 (7 of 12 falls), consistent with duties and local plants.
Caveat to state once (About box). Import data shows origin, not brand, and leaves out locally built machines.

